Shoulder pain is the third most common musculoskeletal complaint after low back and neck pain. It usually means a joint, tendon, or nerve around the shoulder has become irritated, and the neck or upper back often plays a part too. At Thrive Gonstead Chiropractic in Bandar Rimbayu, we look at the whole chain from the upper spine down into the arm before deciding what to treat.

### Why does my shoulder hurt when I lift my arm?

Pain that flares when you lift, reach overhead, or reach behind your back usually points to the rotator cuff. Four small muscles and their tendons, known as the SITS muscles, hold the head of the upper arm bone in the shoulder socket while you move. When those tendons become irritated, every lift pulls on them, and you feel it.

Rotator cuff problems are more likely in certain people. A 2019 meta-analysis found that being over 50, having diabetes, and doing repeated overhead work were each linked to a higher risk (Leong et al., 2019). If any of these apply to you, your shoulder deserves a closer look before the pain becomes long-standing.

### What is actually causing shoulder pain?

Shoulder pain has several common sources, and they often overlap. These are the ones we see most often:

- Rotator cuff irritation, often from repeated overhead work, sport, or a fall onto the shoulder
- Frozen shoulder (adhesive capsulitis), where the joint capsule thickens and tightens
- Neck-related referral, where an irritated nerve in the lower neck sends pain into the shoulder and down the arm
- Upper back stiffness, where a restricted mid-back changes how the shoulder blade moves

Each one can feel like the same deep ache from the outside. Two people with pain in the same spot can need very different care, so guessing at the cause rarely works. The assessment comes first, every time.

The nerves that supply the arm leave the spine in the neck and upper back, then travel through the shoulder region. A review of shoulder and cervical spine pain found evidence that spinal problems can produce shoulder pain, and that shoulder problems can produce neck pain (Systematic review of shoulder and cervical spine referral patterns, PMC7076593). In one case series, about 4% of patients presenting with shoulder pain turned out to have isolated cervical radiculopathy (Roberson, 2015, cited in the same review). This is why we check the spine before we focus only on the joint that hurts.

Frozen shoulder affects roughly 2% to 5% of the general population, and most people diagnosed with it are women between 40 and 60 (Adhesive Capsulitis: Diagnosis and Management, American Family Physician, 2019). It is also more common in people with diabetes, with prevalence reported around 20% to 30% in diabetic populations (Le et al., 2017). Stiffness is what sets it apart, because you lose range of motion even when you are not moving the arm.

### How long does frozen shoulder last?

This is where the evidence needs honesty. The traditional description is three phases: painful, stiff, and recovery, with full recovery expected over time. A 2017 systematic review examined that theory and concluded the evidence for full recovery without treatment is weak and contradictory (Natural history of frozen shoulder: fact or fiction?, Physiotherapy, 2017). Some studies report most patients regaining movement within a few years, but the timeline varies widely, which is why we track progress rather than promise a date.

### How does Gonstead chiropractic address shoulder pain?

Gonstead chiropractic follows a set sequence, so the assessment narrows down where the problem sits. Here is how we work through it:

1. We take a full history, including any fall, injury, or repetitive task that started the pain.
1. We check how the shoulder, neck, and upper back move, and how you hold yourself when standing and sitting.
1. We use static and motion palpation to feel which spinal joints are stiff, tender, or moving poorly.
1. We use the nervoscope to check for temperature and tension changes along the spine, which often point to an irritated nerve segment.
1. We adjust the one joint most involved in the problem, rather than adjusting broadly across the whole spine.

The specificity is the point. A broad adjustment treats the whole spine a little, while a Gonstead adjustment aims at the segment the findings point to.

### What does the research say about chiropractic for shoulder pain?

Chiropractic research on shoulder pain is promising but still limited. A 2010 systematic review found that the evidence for chiropractic management of shoulder pain rested mainly on case reports, case series, and a single small controlled trial, and it called for better-designed studies (Pribicevic et al., 2010). A 2017 review of thrust manipulation found that most trials reported pain reduction, but in several randomised trials the active treatment performed about the same as a sham, so the evidence could neither confirm nor rule out a benefit (Minkalis et al., 2017).

That is why we do not rely on adjustments alone. The results most people get come from combining the adjustment with exercise, posture changes, and a plan you can follow at home.

### What does your first visit involve?

Your first visit at our clinic typically takes 45 to 60 minutes. Most of that time goes into the history and assessment, so the adjustment comes only after we know what we are treating.

We will also show you the stretches and posture changes we want you to start at home. Those small habits make a real difference between visits.

### What can you expect from treatment?

Acute shoulder pain from a recent strain or irritation often eases within the first few visits. Most episodes of non-traumatic shoulder pain have favourable outcomes, and imaging usually does not change management when there are no red flags (Non-traumatic shoulder pain in general practice, NPS MedicineWise).

Frozen shoulder and long-standing rotator cuff problems take longer. They respond best when you do the home exercises between visits. A torn tendon may still need imaging and rehabilitation alongside care, and we will tell you honestly if we think your shoulder needs something beyond what we provide.

### When should you see a doctor first?

Some shoulder pain is not a job for a chiropractor. Shoulder pain can be referred from the heart, so cardiac symptoms come first. Get medical care straight away if your shoulder pain comes with any of these:

- Chest pressure, shortness of breath, sweating, or nausea
- Pain that appears suddenly without any injury, or pain that comes on with exertion and eases with rest
- Pain after a serious fall or accident, or a shoulder that looks out of shape
- Fever, heat, redness, or swelling around the joint
- Weakness or numbness spreading down the arm
- Pain that wakes you at night and does not ease with rest

Heart attacks can present without classic chest pain. Up to one-third of people with acute coronary syndrome do not have typical chest pain, and they may describe arm, shoulder, neck, or jaw discomfort instead (Schmitt, NPACE clinical highlight). If you are in doubt, see a doctor first. We would rather you be checked than wait, and we are happy to see you once you have been cleared.

### Frequently asked questions about shoulder pain

**Can chiropractic help frozen shoulder?**\
Chiropractic may ease pain and improve movement in frozen shoulder, but the research is limited and we do not promise a cure. Gentle joint work combined with home stretching is the approach we use. Recovery is usually slow, so expect weeks to months rather than days.

**Can a neck problem cause shoulder pain?**\
Yes. Irritated nerves in the lower neck can send pain into the shoulder, upper arm, and sometimes the hand, even when the shoulder joint is healthy. Checking the neck is part of every shoulder assessment we do at TGC.

**How many visits will I need?**\
It depends on how long the shoulder has been painful and how much it has changed. Acute pain often improves within a few visits, while long-standing stiffness takes longer. We will set a review point after the first few visits so you know whether the plan is working.

**Why does my shoulder hurt when I sleep on it?**\
Lying on the shoulder compresses the tendons and joint, and a long night in one position can leave the area stiff and sore. Changing your sleeping position and using a pillow to support the arm often helps. Pain that wakes you repeatedly should be checked by a doctor.

Shoulder pain that lasts more than a few weeks deserves a proper look, and the cause is often easier to address than it feels. We will walk you through what we find and what your options are before you decide anything. If you are in Bandar Rimbayu or the surrounding area, book a visit with TGC and we will work out what your shoulder is telling you. Does that make sense?

